        <body>
            <div xml:id="d265">

                <pb facs="0472" n="1432" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1432"/>

                <head>No. 978.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Curry.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-01-26"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 26,
                        1888</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 262.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I desire to call your attention to a case
                    which appears to be clearly in contravention of the commercial agreement with
                    Spain which has lately been extended to June 30 next.</p>
                <p>The American brig <hi rendition="#italic">J.W. Parker</hi> (John W. Kane, master)
                    entered at Zaza, Cuba, from a Spanish port in Porto Rico in ballast. On clearing
                    from the former port with a cargo of mahogany, the master was compelled to pay,
                    under protest, a duty of $1 per ton on the register tonnage of his vessel.</p>
                <p>No Spanish vessel would be compelled to pay such an amount; indeed, as shown by
                    Mr. Williams’s No. 340, of January 28, 1886 (see <ref
                        target="frus:frus1886/pg_783">Foreign Relations, 1886, pp. 783</ref>, <ref
                        target="frus:frus1886/pg_784">784</ref>), the charge on a Spanish vessel
                    under similar circumstances would be 25 cents per ton.</p>
                <p>Copies of the papers are inclosed, and you are requested to present the case to
                    the Spanish Government and to ask the repayment of the excess of duties exacted
                    from Captain Kane.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>




